# The first dates of the things in the pictures are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts.
lesson history-reading-an-old-picture v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=8 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Reading an old picture"
  goal "Find what is the same and what is different in two pictures of one place, and use the clues in a picture to say when it was made."

  look {
    scene 32x33 {
      column pics at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        thenandnow a year=1900 sign=1 things=["horse and cart", "bicycle", "hot-air balloon"]
        thenandnow b year=2026 sign=1 things=["motor car", "bicycle", "mobile phone"]
      }
    }
    say """
      These are two pictures of the same street, one in 1900 and one today. The houses are the
      same. The bicycle is in both, since people have ridden bicycles since before 1900. The horse
      and cart has gone, and cars and phones are new. When a picture has no date, its newest thing
      is the best clue: the picture cannot be older than that thing.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.two-pictures q=0 k=0
    }
    show history.two-pictures q=1 k=1 easy-q=0 easy-k=1 hard-q=1 hard-k=0
    show history.picture-clue k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.picture-mistake k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level medium hard {
      show history.two-pictures q=1 k=0 hard-q=2 hard-k=0
    }
    show history.guess-the-year k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.picture-mistake k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show history.two-pictures q=2 k=1 easy-q=1 easy-k=1 level=medium hard-q=2 hard-k=1
  }
  story {
    level hard {
      show history.picture-clue k=0 level=medium
    }
    level medium hard {
      show history.guess-the-year k=2 hard-k=1 level=medium
    }
    level easy {
      show history.picture-clue k=1 level=medium
      show history.picture-mistake k=2 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.same-street-changed k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.old-photo
  }
  remember {
    say "A picture cannot be older than the newest thing in it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a picture is evidence: what is in it tells when it was made. The way in finds
    what two pictures of one street share; the core finds what is new, which picture is older, the
    mistake in an artist's picture of a given year and the year an undated picture could be. The
    wrong idea to watch for is dating a picture by its houses, which may stand for centuries; the
    best clue is the newest thing. If the child is stuck, ask of each thing "could Great-grandma
    have seen one as a child?" The three-star task is for you to mark with the child: an old
    photograph of your family or your street, and what is the same and what is different today.
    England's key stage 1 history asks pupils to "use a wide vocabulary of everyday historical
    terms" and to "understand some of the ways in which we find out about the past", and a picture
    is the first of those ways.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example finds what is in both pictures, every two-picture question asks what they share, the mistakes are far newer than the year, and the undated picture has a bicycle in it."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the two-picture questions ask what is only new and which picture is older, the mistakes are only a few decades out, and the undated picture's newest thing is the aeroplane."
  }
}
